### What is HTTP/3 in JDK HttpClient?

HTTP/3 support in the JDK HttpClient runs over QUIC, a transport protocol built on UDP rather than TCP.

### How is QUIC different from TCP?

| Aspect | TCP (HTTP/2) | QUIC (HTTP/3) |
|---|---|---|
| Transport | TCP (connection-oriented) | UDP (connectionless) |
| Connection setup | 3 round-trips (TCP + TLS) | 1 round-trip (combined) |
| Resumption | Not built-in | 0-RTT with cached params |
| Head-of-line blocking | One lost packet stalls all streams | Each stream independent |
| Connection migration | New handshake on IP change | Survives IP changes |
| Multiplexing | HTTP/2 multiplexing | Stream-level independence |

### How do you use HTTP/3 in Java?

```java
package com.bank.client;

import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;

public class BankApiClient {

    private final HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
        .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_3)  // prefer HTTP/3
        .build();

    public String getAccountBalance(String accountId) throws Exception {
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
            .uri(URI.create("https://api.nationalbank.in/accounts/" + accountId + "/balance"))
            .GET()
            .build();

        HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request,
            HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
        return response.body();
        // If server supports HTTP/3: uses QUIC (faster setup, no HOL blocking)
        // If not: automatically falls back to HTTP/2 over TCP
    }
}
```

### Why does 0-RTT matter for banking?

In microservice architectures, services make many short-lived HTTP calls. Each call's latency includes connection setup time. With TCP+TLS, that is 3 round-trips (roughly 30ms on a 10ms network). With QUIC, that drops to 1 round-trip or even 0-RTT for resumed connections.

For a transfer that calls 5 downstream services, connection setup overhead drops from 150ms to 50ms or less.

### Why does no head-of-line blocking matter?

A mobile banking app fetches account summary, recent transactions, and offers in parallel over one connection. With HTTP/2 over TCP, a dropped packet on the transactions stream stalls the summary and offers streams until the packet is retransmitted. With HTTP/3, each stream is independent at the transport layer.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- HTTP/3 is a network-efficiency improvement, not an API change. Your request/response code stays the same.
- Evaluate HTTP/3 where connection setup cost and packet loss dominate latency: mobile apps, cross-region calls, high-frequency API calls.
- Keep HTTP/2 fallback for servers that do not speak QUIC.
- QUIC runs on UDP port 443; ensure firewalls allow UDP traffic.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does HTTP/3 change the Java API? (No. Same HttpClient, HttpRequest, HttpResponse. Only the transport changes.)
- When does HTTP/3 NOT help? (On reliable, low-latency networks where TCP overhead is negligible.)
- What is connection migration? (QUIC connections use a connection ID instead of IP:port, so they survive WiFi-to-mobile handoffs.)
